<% content_for :title, "On Depression | Bipolar Sober" %>

<style>
  .depression-page {
    background: #080808;
    color: #fff;
    font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;
  }

  .depression-page *,
  .depression-page *::before,
  .depression-page *::after {
    box-sizing: border-box;
  }

  .depression-hero {
    min-height: 85vh;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 80px 6%;
    background-color: #080808;
    background-image:
      linear-gradient(90deg, rgba(8, 8, 8, .55), transparent),
      url("<%= asset_path('bipolar_depression_desktop.png') %>");
    background-size: cover;
    background-position: center;
  }

  .depression-hero-content {
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 1200px;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  .depression-eyebrow {
    max-width: 440px;
    margin: 0 0 28px;
    font-size: 12px;
    font-weight: 800;
    line-height: 1.6;
    letter-spacing: .12em;
  }

  .depression-hero h1 {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: clamp(48px, 7.5vw, 112px);
    font-weight: 900;
    font-style: italic;
    line-height: .95;
    letter-spacing: -.055em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .depression-hero h1 span {
    display: block;
  }

  .depression-intro {
    margin: 32px 0 0;
    font-size: clamp(15px, 1.6vw, 22px);
    font-weight: 800;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  .depression-statement {
    padding: 48px 6%;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .depression-statement p {
    max-width: 1000px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    color: #eaff00;
    font-size: clamp(26px, 4vw, 52px);
    font-weight: 900;
    font-style: italic;
    line-height: 1.1;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .depression-section {
    padding: 72px 6%;
  }

  .depression-section-inner {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, .85fr) minmax(0, 1.15fr);
    gap: 60px;
    max-width: 1200px;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  .depression-number {
    margin: 0 0 22px;
    font-size: 12px;
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: .12em;
  }

  .depression-section h2 {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: clamp(36px, 4.5vw, 68px);
    font-weight: 900;
    font-style: italic;
    line-height: .95;
    letter-spacing: -.045em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .depression-copy {
    font-size: 17px;
    line-height: 1.8;
  }

  .depression-copy p {
    margin: 0 0 22px;
  }

  .depression-copy p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .depression-copy ul {
    margin: 0 0 24px;
    padding-left: 22px;
  }

  .depression-copy li {
    margin-bottom: 12px;
  }

  .depression-pink {
    background: #ff1493;
    color: #080808;
  }

  .depression-yellow {
    background: #eaff00;
    color: #080808;
  }

  .depression-purple {
    background: #8a00ff;
    color: #fff;
  }

  .depression-black {
    background: #080808;
    color: #fff;
  }

  .depression-white {
    background: #fff;
    color: #080808;
  }

  .depression-takeaway {
    padding-top: 22px;
    border-top: 3px solid currentColor;
    font-weight: 800;
  }

  .depression-footer {
    padding: 40px 6%;
    border-top: 1px solid #444;
  }

  .depression-footer-inner {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 24px;
    max-width: 1200px;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  .depression-footer a {
    color: #eaff00;
    font-size: 14px;
    font-weight: 800;
    line-height: 1.5;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .depression-footer a:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  .depression-footer a:focus-visible {
    outline: 3px solid #ff1493;
    outline-offset: 6px;
  }

  @media (max-width: 767px) {
    .depression-hero {
      min-height: 88svh;
      align-items: flex-start;
      padding: 48px 6% 60px;
      background-image:
        url("<%= asset_path('bipolar_depression_mobile.png') %>");
      background-position: center bottom;
    }

    .depression-eyebrow {
      margin-bottom: 24px;
      font-size: 10px;
    }

    .depression-hero h1 {
      font-size: clamp(36px, 11.5vw, 86px);
    }

    .depression-intro {
      margin-top: 24px;
      font-size: 14px;
    }

    .depression-section {
      padding: 48px 6%;
    }

    .depression-section-inner {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 28px;
    }

    .depression-section h2 {
      font-size: clamp(36px, 10vw, 58px);
    }

    .depression-copy {
      font-size: 16px;
      line-height: 1.8;
    }

    .depression-number {
      margin-bottom: 16px;
    }
  }
</style>

<main class="depression-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="depression-hero" aria-labelledby="depression-title">
    <div class="depression-hero-content">

      <p class="depression-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / BIPOLAR / DEPRESSION
      </p>

      <h1 id="depression-title">
        <span>ON</span>
        <span>DEPRESSION.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="depression-intro">
        THE WEIGHT. THE NOTHING.<br>
        THE DAYS THAT KEEP COMING.<br>
        GETTING THROUGH THE FUCKING THING.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- OPENING -->
  <section class="depression-statement" aria-label="Opening thought">
    <p>
      YOU DO NOT HAVE TO FEEL HOPEFUL<br>
      TO DESERVE HELP.
    </p>
  </section>


  <!-- 01 / THE LONG HAUL -->
  <section class="depression-section depression-purple">
    <div class="depression-section-inner">

      <div class="depression-section-heading">
        <p class="depression-number">01 / THE LONG HAUL</p>

        <h2>
          WHEN THE<br>
          LOW DOESN’T<br>
          LET UP.
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="depression-copy">
        <p>
          Sometimes depression means months of waking up and finding
          the same weight waiting for you. Four months in, you can
          get tired of explaining that you are still struggling.
          You are tired of the episode. Tired of waiting. Tired of
          having to get through another day that feels like this.
        </p>

        <p>
          Maybe you feel painfully sad. Maybe you feel almost nothing.
          Things you usually care about seem distant, and ordinary
          tasks take more effort than anyone can see. Then comes
          the guilt about everything you think you should be doing.
        </p>

        <p class="depression-takeaway">
          Your capacity deserves to be taken seriously, including
          when the struggle has lasted longer than other people understand.
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 02 / THOUGHTS -->
  <section class="depression-section depression-yellow">
    <div class="depression-section-inner">

      <div class="depression-section-heading">
        <p class="depression-number">02 / CHECK THE VERDICT</p>

        <h2>
          IT FEELS<br>
          FOREVER.
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="depression-copy">
        <p>
          When you have felt awful for months, “this will never change”
          can seem like a reasonable conclusion. The feeling is real.
          The prediction still deserves questioning. How you feel today
          cannot tell you everything about your future.
        </p>

        <p>
          Notice when your mind turns difficulty into identity:
          “I couldn’t finish that” becomes “I’m useless.”
          “I need help” becomes “I’m a burden.”
          Try describing what happened without sentencing your
          entire self.
        </p>

        <p>
          You do not need a bright, inspirational replacement thought.
          “I’m having a hard time, and I need support” can be accurate
          enough. Give yourself a statement you can actually believe.
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 03 / SMALL ACTIONS -->
  <section class="depression-section depression-pink">
    <div class="depression-section-inner">

      <div class="depression-section-heading">
        <p class="depression-number">03 / MAKE IT SMALLER</p>

        <h2>
          ONE THING.<br>
          THEN SEE.
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="depression-copy">
        <p>
          The whole day may feel impossible. Bring the task down
          to something you can attempt with the energy available.
          Eat something simple. Change your shirt. Send one message.
          Put one dish in the sink.
        </p>

        <ul>
          <li>Choose one manageable action instead of a complete overhaul.</li>
          <li>Break tasks into smaller pieces and allow breaks.</li>
          <li>Adapt expectations around pain, exhaustion, and your actual limits.</li>
          <li>Let someone help with the parts you cannot manage alone.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          Sometimes a small action gives you a little momentum.
          Sometimes it takes everything you have. Its value does
          not depend on whether you suddenly feel better afterward.
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 04 / SELF-COMPASSION -->
  <section class="depression-section depression-black">
    <div class="depression-section-inner">

      <div class="depression-section-heading">
        <p class="depression-number">04 / DROP THE EXTRA WEIGHT</p>

        <h2>
          YOU’RE<br>
          ALREADY<br>
          CARRYING<br>
          ENOUGH.
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="depression-copy">
        <p>
          Depression is exhausting enough without spending the day
          calling yourself lazy, weak, or a fucking disappointment.
          Notice the suffering already here and the punishment you
          add to it. You can work on the second without pretending
          you can simply switch off the first.
        </p>

        <p>
          When it helps, come back to something immediate: the feel
          of the chair, a sound in the room, the next sip of water.
          You can acknowledge “this is painful” without solving
          your entire life in that moment.
        </p>

        <p class="depression-takeaway">
          You remain worthy of care on the days when your main
          accomplishment is getting through.
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 05 / SUPPORT -->
  <section class="depression-section depression-white">
    <div class="depression-section-inner">

      <div class="depression-section-heading">
        <p class="depression-number">05 / LET SOMEONE IN</p>

        <h2>
          MAKE THE<br>
          ASK<br>
          SPECIFIC.
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="depression-copy">
        <p>
          Explaining everything can feel like another impossible task.
          Start with the practical help you need. Someone does not
          have to understand every part of the episode to bring food,
          sit beside you, or help you make a call.
        </p>

        <ul>
          <li>“Can you check in with me tonight?”</li>
          <li>“Can you help me sort out dinner?”</li>
          <li>“Can you sit with me while I handle this appointment?”</li>
          <li>“I don’t need advice right now. I could use some company.”</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          You can ask for quiet company. You can say that you are
          still struggling even if you have said it before.
          Your need for support does not expire because the episode
          has lasted a long time.
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 06 / ACCEPTANCE -->
  <section class="depression-section depression-purple">
    <div class="depression-section-inner">

      <div class="depression-section-heading">
        <p class="depression-number">06 / WORK WITH TODAY</p>

        <h2>
          WHAT IS<br>
          AVAILABLE<br>
          RIGHT NOW?
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="depression-copy">
        <p>
          You may not be able to produce motivation, relief, or a
          different mood on demand. Ask what is available today:
          a smaller task, an honest conversation, some rest,
          or help getting through the next hour.
        </p>

        <p>
          You can approach the day as a problem to work with:
          given these limits, how can you respond with care and
          resourcefulness? There is no requirement to turn the pain
          into a lesson or prove how much you can endure.
        </p>

        <p>
          Accepting your current limits can help you choose a workable
          next step. It leaves room for your circumstances to change
          and for other people to help.
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 07 / CARE AND HOPE -->
  <section class="depression-section depression-yellow">
    <div class="depression-section-inner">

      <div class="depression-section-heading">
        <p class="depression-number">07 / KEEP ROOM FOR CHANGE</p>

        <h2>
          YOU DON’T<br>
          HAVE TO<br>
          DO THIS<br>
          ALONE.
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="depression-copy">
        <p>
          Let your treatment team know when depression persists,
          worsens, or makes everyday care difficult. Tell them
          what your days actually look like, including the things
          you have stopped being able to do.
        </p>

        <p>
          If you are thinking about suicide, feel unable to stay safe,
          or cannot meet basic needs, seek urgent help. You deserve
          support before things become unbearable.
        </p>

        <p>
          Hope can be difficult to feel from inside a long episode.
          For now, it can look like keeping an appointment, accepting
          a meal, or telling someone the truth about how you are doing.
        </p>

        <p class="depression-takeaway">
          Leave some room for a future you cannot clearly picture yet.
          You deserve care while you get there.
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- NAVIGATION -->
  <nav class="depression-footer" aria-label="Bipolar topic navigation">
    <div class="depression-footer-inner">
      <%= link_to "← BACK TO BIPOLAR", bipolar_path %>
      <%= link_to "NEXT: ON MIXED EPISODES →", bipolar_mixed_episodes_path %>
    </div>
  </nav>

</main>